    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I implemented\n\n**Structured address form** for `POST /api/owners`, keeping the flat `address` fully backward-compatible.\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** (`OwnerFields` schema) \u2014 added `addressLine1` (min 1 / max 255) and optional `addressLine2` (max 255), and **removed `address` from the `required` list** so a request supplying only structured fields passes schema validation. `address` is now documented as the composed/normalized value on response. Since `Owner` is `allOf OwnerFields`, the response DTO gains `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` automatically.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added persistent `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields (`@Column(name = \"address_line1\"/\"address_line2\")`) with getters/setters. MapStruct maps them through both directions by name.\n\n3. **`AddressNormalizer.java`** \u2014 added a small `compose(normalizedLine1, normalizedLine2)` method: line1, plus a single space and line2 when line2 is present. Kept the existing single-line `normalize` as the one normalization primitive it reuses.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 replaced the single normalize call with a private `normalizeAddress(dto)` helper that normalizes both structured lines in place and resolves the effective `address`: when `addressLine1` is non-blank it composes from the normalized lines (structured preferred), otherwise it normalizes the flat `address`. Because this runs before `OwnerFieldsValidator`, the existing \"address required\" check now enforces exactly \"non-blank `addressLine1` **or** flat `address`, plus city\" \u2014 no validator change needed.\n\n5. **`schema.sql`** (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus postgres/mysql/h2 for consistency) \u2014 added nullable `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to `owners`.\n\n## Design notes\n\n- The household hash (`HouseholdKey`, keyed on last name + postcode), postcode validation (`PostcodeValidator`, city + postcode) and locality (`RegionResolver`, postcode + city) all read the structured `postcode`/`city` fields directly \u2014 which are supplied identically in both the flat and structured forms \u2014 so they already satisfy the \"use structured fields, fall back to flat address\" requirement without change.\n- Each new piece stayed in its natural home (schema, entity, normalizer, controller orchestration); nothing became a catch-all, and normalization/composition logic is reused rather than duplicated.",
